DEEP[26]=("<i>Na buddhi-bhedaṃ janayet</i>, “let him not create a split in understanding”. <i>Ajñānāṃ karma-saṅginām</i>, “of the ignorant attached to action”. <i>Joṣayet sarva-karmāṇi</i>, “let him engage them in all actions”. <i>Vidvān yuktaḥ samācaran</i>, “the wise one, himself acting with discipline”.",
 "<i>Buddhi-bheda</i>, a split in understanding, is the danger of teaching too far ahead of a person’s readiness. Commentators give the example of telling a hard-working farmer that all work is illusion: he may stop working without gaining any wisdom. Śaṅkara says the wise should not unsettle faith in action among those not ready for knowledge. Rāmānuja says the wise should show karma yoga by doing it. Many modern teachers apply the verse to education and parenting: build on what a person can do, don’t knock away the ladder they are standing on. Vivekananda said: help, don’t fight; assimilate, don’t destroy.",
 "Before sharing a big idea, ask whether it will help the listener or just unsettle them. Sometimes example teaches best.")
DEEP[27]=("<i>Prakṛteḥ kriyamāṇāni guṇaiḥ karmāṇi sarvaśaḥ</i>, “all actions are being done by the guṇas of nature”. <i>Ahaṅkāra-vimūḍhātmā</i>, “one deluded by ego”. <i>Kartāham iti manyate</i>, “thinks ‘I am the doer’”.",
 "<i>Ahaṅkāra</i>, literally “I-maker”, is the sense of being a separate doer. In Sāṅkhya philosophy it is a product of nature, not of the Self. Commentators of every school treat this verse as central. Śaṅkara: the Self is actionless; all action belongs to nature, and claiming it is ignorance. Rāmānuja: the self is an agent, but its agency depends on the body and ultimately on the Lord; thinking “I alone do this” is the error. Madhva: God is the independent doer; the self is a dependent doer. All agree that the proud “I did it” is a mistake. The idea returns in 13.29 and 18.14–16.",
 "Next time you succeed, list everything that made it possible. You will find “I” was one cause among many.")
DEEP[28]=("<i>Tattva-vit tu mahā-bāho</i>, “but the knower of truth, mighty-armed one”. <i>Guṇa-karma-vibhāgayoḥ</i>, “of the divisions of guṇas and actions”. <i>Guṇā guṇeṣu vartanta</i>, “guṇas move among guṇas”. <i>Iti matvā na sajjate</i>, “knowing thus, is not attached”.",
 "<i>Guṇā guṇeṣu vartante</i> is a famous formula. Commentators explain: the senses, mind and body are made of the guṇas; so are the objects they meet. When eyes see a form, it is nature meeting nature. The Self watches. Śaṅkara takes this as the knower’s vision of non-agency. Rāmānuja says the wise know that the self is different from the guṇas and their play. The idea anticipates chapter 14, where the one beyond the guṇas sits like a neutral witness, knowing “the guṇas alone are acting” (14.23).",
 "When a mood or craving rises, try the phrase “nature is moving in nature”. It gives a moment of freedom.")
DEEP[29]=("<i>Prakṛter guṇa-sammūḍhāḥ</i>, “deluded by nature’s guṇas”. <i>Sajjante guṇa-karmasu</i>, “are attached to the works of the guṇas”. <i>Tān akṛtsna-vido mandān</i>, “those of partial knowledge, slow”. <i>Kṛtsna-vin na vicālayet</i>, “let the knower of the whole not unsettle them”.",
 "<i>Akṛtsna-vid</i>, “knower of the partial”, and <i>kṛtsna-vid</i>, “knower of the whole”: the contrast is gentle. Those who know only part are not condemned, just not yet complete. Commentators note the repeated instruction (3.26, 3.29): the Gītā cares about teaching with tact. Śaṅkara stresses that the knower should guide by example, not by unsettling. Rāmānuja says the partial knower should still be encouraged in good action. Many modern readers find the verse a lesson in humility and respect for others’ pace.",
 "Everyone knows only part. Treat others’ partial understanding the way you hope they treat yours.")
DEEP[30]=("<i>Mayi sarvāṇi karmāṇi sannyasya</i>, “offering all actions to me”. <i>Adhyātma-cetasā</i>, “with the mind on the Self”. <i>Nirāśīr nirmamo bhūtvā</i>, “free of hope and of ‘mine’”. <i>Yudhyasva vigata-jvaraḥ</i>, “fight, the fever gone”.",
 "This verse is the climax of chapter 3’s teaching on action. Commentators note that <i>sannyasya</i>, “renouncing”, does not mean giving up action but offering it. True renunciation, the Gītā will say, is inner (5.1–12, 18.2). <i>Nirāśīḥ</i>, free of expectation; <i>nirmamaḥ</i>, free of possessiveness, echoing 2.71. <i>Vigata-jvaraḥ</i>, the fever of anxiety gone. Rāmānuja calls this the essence of karma yoga as devotion. Śaṅkara reads “me” as the Lord who is the inner Self. Madhva stresses offering to Viṣṇu. The verse anticipates 9.27 and 18.57, where the offering of all actions becomes the core of bhakti.",
 "Hand over the outcome before you start. Doing the work then becomes lighter, and the fever of worry cools.")
DEEP[31]=("<i>Ye me matam idaṃ nityam</i>, “those who constantly follow this view of mine”. <i>Anutiṣṭhanti mānavāḥ</i>, “practise it”. <i>Śraddhāvanto’nasūyantaḥ</i>, “with faith, not carping”. <i>Mucyante te’pi karmabhiḥ</i>, “they too are freed from actions”.",
 "<i>Śraddhā</i>, faith, is a key word that becomes the theme of chapter 17. Commentators define it not as blind belief but as trust that leads to practice. <i>Anasūya</i>, free of carping, means not looking for faults in the teaching or the teacher. Śaṅkara says those who follow this teaching are freed gradually, through purity of mind. Rāmānuja says they are freed from the bondage of past karma. The word <i>api</i>, “too”, is generous: not only great sages but ordinary followers are freed.",
 "Give a good teaching a fair trial in practice. Trust that is tested by experience grows stronger.")
DEEP[32]=("<i>Ye tv etad abhyasūyanto</i>, “but those who find fault with this”. <i>Nānutiṣṭhanti me matam</i>, “and do not practise my teaching”. <i>Sarva-jñāna-vimūḍhāṃs tān</i>, “deluded in all knowledge”. <i>Viddhi naṣṭān acetasaḥ</i>, “know them as lost and senseless”.",
 "Commentators are careful with this verse. It is aimed at <i>asūyā</i>, malicious fault-finding, not at sincere questioning. The Gītā itself is a long dialogue of questions. Arjuna asks again and again, and Kṛṣṇa welcomes it. In 18.63 Kṛṣṇa says, “reflect on it fully, then do as you wish”. Śaṅkara says the fault-finders are deluded because they reject the means to their own good. Rāmānuja says they lose the chance of freedom. The warning is against a closed mind, not against an inquiring one.",
 "Questions open doors; contempt closes them. Ask hard questions, but leave room for an answer.")
DEEP[33]=("<i>Sadṛśaṃ ceṣṭate svasyāḥ prakṛter jñānavān api</i>, “even the wise act in keeping with their own nature”. <i>Prakṛtiṃ yānti bhūtāni</i>, “beings follow nature”. <i>Nigrahaḥ kiṃ kariṣyati</i>, “what will repression do?”.",
 "<i>Nigraha</i>, forcible restraint or repression. Commentators note that this sounds like fatalism but is followed at once by advice (3.34): do not fall under likes and dislikes. Śaṅkara says nature here means the tendencies from past actions, <i>saṃskāras</i>. They are strong, but one can refuse to be driven by them. Rāmānuja says the force of habit makes karma yoga easier than sudden renunciation, because it works with nature rather than against it. The verse connects to 18.59–60, where Kṛṣṇa tells Arjuna that his nature will compel him to fight anyway.",
 "Work with your nature, not against it. Redirecting a strong tendency is easier than crushing it.")
DEEP[34]=("<i>Indriyasyendriyasyārthe</i>, “in the object of each sense”. <i>Rāga-dveṣau vyavasthitau</i>, “liking and disliking are set”. <i>Tayor na vaśam āgacchet</i>, “do not come under their power”. <i>Tau hy asya paripanthinau</i>, “for they are his robbers on the road”.",
 "<i>Rāga</i> and <i>dveṣa</i>, attraction and aversion, are named in Patañjali’s Yoga Sūtra (2.7–8) among the five afflictions. The Buddha’s teaching names <i>lobha</i> and <i>dosa</i>, greed and hatred, as two of the three roots of unwholesome action. Commentators note how practical the verse is. We cannot stop the senses from liking and disliking; that is how they work. But we can stop those likes from steering us. Śaṅkara says freedom lies in noticing them and not obeying. The image of <i>paripanthin</i>, highwaymen, pictures life as a journey with ambushes at every turn.",
 "When you feel a strong pull or push, name it: “that is liking” or “that is disliking”. Then decide freely.")
DEEP[35]=("<i>Śreyān sva-dharmo viguṇaḥ</i>, “better one’s own dharma, though imperfect”. <i>Para-dharmāt sv-anuṣṭhitāt</i>, “than another’s, well done”. <i>Sva-dharme nidhanaṃ śreyaḥ</i>, “better death in one’s own dharma”. <i>Para-dharmo bhayāvahaḥ</i>, “another’s dharma brings fear”.",
 "Arjuna, a warrior, was drawn to the life of a beggar-monk (2.5). Kṛṣṇa says: that is not your path. The same teaching returns in 18.47, with the addition that one who acts by one’s own nature incurs no sin. Commentators read <i>svadharma</i> in different ways. Traditionally, it meant duties of one’s station in life. Many modern readers, including Vivekananda and Aurobindo, read it as one’s own inner calling. Gandhi took it as a call to serve in one’s own setting rather than imitate others. All agree the verse warns against borrowed lives.",
 "Comparing ourselves with others makes us restless. Doing our own work, even clumsily, brings peace.")
DEEP[36]=("Arjuna’s question begins with <i>atha kena prayukto’yam</i>, “then impelled by what does a person…”. <i>Anicchann api vārṣṇeya</i>, “even unwilling, Vārṣṇeya”. <i>Balād iva niyojitaḥ</i>, “as if forced”.",
 "This is Arjuna’s third question. Commentators admire its universality. Duryodhana in the Mahābhārata is quoted in a famous verse: <i>jānāmi dharmaṃ na ca me pravṛttiḥ, jānāmy adharmaṃ na ca me nivṛttiḥ</i>, “I know dharma but cannot do it; I know adharma but cannot stop”. That verse comes from a later text, the Pāṇḍava Gītā, but it is widely quoted. St Paul says something similar in Romans 7: “the good that I would, I do not”. Śaṅkara notes that Arjuna has understood 3.34 and now asks for the root cause. The answer comes in one word: desire.",
 "If you keep doing what you don’t want to do, don’t only blame willpower. Look for the hidden driver.")
DEEP[37]=("<i>Kāma eṣa krodha eṣa</i>, “it is desire, it is anger”. <i>Rajo-guṇa-samudbhavaḥ</i>, “born of the guṇa of rajas”. <i>Mahāśano mahā-pāpmā</i>, “all-devouring, greatly sinful”. <i>Viddhy enam iha vairiṇam</i>, “know this as the enemy here”.",
 "Kṛṣṇa answers in one breath and names the enemy precisely. Commentators note that desire and anger are one force: anger is desire frustrated (2.62). <i>Rajas</i>, the guṇa of passion and restlessness, is their source (14.7, 14.12). <i>Mahāśana</i>, “great eater”: desire consumes everything given to it and asks for more. Śaṅkara says desire is the enemy because it drives one to wrong action. Rāmānuja says it is the soul’s chief obstacle. In 16.21, desire, anger and greed are called the three gates of hell.",
 "Track your anger back. Often you will find a desire that was blocked. Deal with the desire, and the anger cools.")
DEEP[38]=("<i>Dhūmenāvriyate vahnir</i>, “as fire is covered by smoke”; <i>yathādarśo malena ca</i>, “a mirror by dirt”; <i>yatholbenāvṛto garbhaḥ</i>, “an embryo by the womb”; <i>tathā tenedam āvṛtam</i>, “so this is covered by that”.",
 "Three images, three degrees of covering. Commentators often map them to the three guṇas. Smoke barely hides a fire and a breeze clears it; this is the slight covering of a sattvic mind. Dust on a mirror needs effort to wipe, the rajasic mind. The womb fully hides the child until birth, the tamasic mind, which needs time and patience. Śaṅkara keeps it simpler: knowledge is always there, like fire, but desire hides it. The mirror image is a favourite in many traditions. Zen and Sufi teachers also speak of polishing the mirror of the heart.",
 "Wisdom is not missing, only covered. The work is clearing, not adding.")
DEEP[39]=("<i>Āvṛtaṃ jñānam etena</i>, “knowledge is covered by this”. <i>Jñānino nitya-vairiṇā</i>, “the constant enemy of the wise”. <i>Kāma-rūpeṇa kaunteya</i>, “in the form of desire, son of Kuntī”. <i>Duṣpūreṇānalena ca</i>, “a fire hard to fill”.",
 "The Manusmṛti (2.94), and the Mahābhārata in the story of King Yayāti, give the classic simile. Desire is never satisfied by enjoyment; like fire fed with ghee, it only grows. Yayāti, after a thousand years of pleasure, reached this very conclusion. Commentators note that desire is called the “constant enemy of the wise” because the ignorant mistake it for a friend; the wise see its harm. <i>Kāma-rūpa</i> also means “able to take any form”: desire changes shape to tempt. Śaṅkara says it is the cause of every pain the wise see coming.",
 "Notice the cycle: desire, satisfaction, more desire. Stepping out of it brings a quieter joy.")
DEEP[40]=("<i>Indriyāṇi mano buddhir</i>, “senses, mind, intellect”. <i>Asyādhiṣṭhānam ucyate</i>, “are said to be its seat”. <i>Etair vimohayaty eṣa</i>, “through these it deludes”. <i>Jñānam āvṛtya dehinam</i>, “covering knowledge, the embodied one”.",
 "This verse is a map of the enemy’s territory: three strongholds. Commentators explain how desire uses each. It catches the eye through the senses, it settles in the mind as longing, and in the intellect it builds reasons why giving in is right. The last is the most dangerous, because it looks like wisdom. Śaṅkara says knowing the seats of desire is the first step to defeating it. The next verses describe the strategy: start at the outer gate (3.41), then rise through the levels (3.42–43).",
 "When you hear yourself making very clever arguments for something you crave, be suspicious. Desire may be speaking.")
DEEP[41]=("<i>Tasmāt tvam indriyāṇy ādau niyamya</i>, “therefore, first controlling the senses”. <i>Bharatarṣabha</i>, “bull of the Bharatas”. <i>Pāpmānaṃ prajahi hy enam</i>, “destroy this sinful one”. <i>Jñāna-vijñāna-nāśanam</i>, “destroyer of knowledge and realisation”.",
 "<i>Jñāna</i> and <i>vijñāna</i>: Śaṅkara takes the first as knowledge from scripture and teacher, the second as one’s own direct experience of it. Rāmānuja takes them as knowledge of the self and of its distinct nature. The pair returns as the title of chapter 7, <i>Jñāna-Vijñāna Yoga</i>. Commentators note the practical order: start with the senses. One cannot argue with desire in the intellect while letting it pour in through the eyes. Ancient training of students, <i>brahmacarya</i>, began with sense discipline for this reason.",
 "Guard the gates first: what you see, hear, eat. Inner work is easier when the outer doors are watched.")
DEEP[42]=("<i>Indriyāṇi parāṇy āhur</i>, “the senses, they say, are higher”. <i>Indriyebhyaḥ paraṃ manaḥ</i>, “higher than the senses, the mind”. <i>Manasas tu parā buddhir</i>, “higher than the mind, the intellect”. <i>Yo buddheḥ paratas tu saḥ</i>, “and what is beyond the intellect is He”.",
 "The Kaṭha Upaniṣad (1.3.10–11) gives a fuller ladder: beyond the senses are the objects, beyond them the mind, then the intellect, then the great self, then the unmanifest, then the Person, beyond whom there is nothing. The Gītā uses a shorter form. Commentators read <i>saḥ</i>, “He”, as the Self (Śaṅkara) or the individual self (Rāmānuja), or, in some readings, desire itself, which hides even beyond the intellect. Most take it as the Self. The practical meaning: each higher level can govern the lower. When all fail, rest in what is beyond all.",
 "When the senses pull, call on the mind. When the mind wavers, call on reason. When reason tires, remember who you are.")
DEEP[43]=("<i>Evaṃ buddheḥ paraṃ buddhvā</i>, “thus knowing what is beyond the intellect”. <i>Saṃstabhyātmānam ātmanā</i>, “steadying the self by the Self”. <i>Jahi śatruṃ mahā-bāho</i>, “slay the enemy, mighty-armed one”. <i>Kāma-rūpaṃ durāsadam</i>, “in the form of desire, hard to conquer”.",
 "The chapter ends with the same verb Kṛṣṇa used of the outer battle: <i>jahi</i>, “slay”. But the enemy now is desire. Commentators note how the Gītā moves the battlefield inside: the true Kurukṣetra is the heart. Śaṅkara says the lower self, the mind, is steadied by the higher Self through knowledge. Rāmānuja says the mind is steadied by fixing it on the self. Gandhi read the whole Gītā this way: the war is within, and the weapon is selfless action. The chapter colophon names it Karma Yoga, the yoga of action. Chapter 4 will add knowledge, and reveal who the teacher really is.",
 "The hardest victories are inner. Name your enemy (one craving, one habit) and take one step against it today.")
